---
import ProjectLayout from "../../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";
---

<ProjectLayout
	title="Graduate Admission Prediction"
	category="Statistics · Neural Networks · Regression"
	year="2026"
	summary="A comparative modeling study testing whether a neural network adds predictive value over logistic regression on structured graduate-admission data."
>

	<!-- =====================================================
	     01 — RESEARCH QUESTION
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			Research Question
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<p class="eyebrow">
				The broader problem
			</p>

			<h2>
				When does a neural network add value over a simpler statistical model?
			</h2>

			<p class="lead">
				This project compares an artificial neural network with
				logistic regression for classifying applicants with a
				high predicted chance of graduate admission.
			</p>

			<p>
				Both models are trained on the same structured applicant
				features, allowing the comparison to focus on model choice
				rather than differences in the underlying data.
			</p>

			<p>
				The central question is therefore not simply which model
				achieves a higher score, but whether additional model
				complexity produces a meaningful predictive advantage.
			</p>

		</div>

	</div>


	<!-- =====================================================
	     02 — WHY IT MATTERS
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			Why It Matters
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<h2>
				More complex does not always mean more useful.
			</h2>

			<p>
				Neural networks can represent nonlinear relationships,
				but structured tabular datasets do not always require
				high-capacity models.
			</p>

			<p>
				A simpler statistical model may achieve comparable
				predictive performance while remaining easier to train,
				interpret and reproduce.
			</p>

			<p>
				Comparing both approaches on the same prediction task
				provides a practical example of model selection:
				complexity should be justified by measurable improvement.
			</p>

		</div>

	</div>


	<!-- =====================================================
	     03 — BIG QUESTIONS
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			Big Questions
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<p class="eyebrow">
				The analysis in three questions
			</p>

			<h2>
				Three questions guided the comparison.
			</h2>


			<div class="question-grid">

				<div class="question-card">

					<span class="question-number">
						01
					</span>

					<h3>
						Can structured applicant characteristics identify high-admission-chance cases?
					</h3>

					<p>
						Use academic, institutional and research-related
						features to construct a binary classification task
						from the original admission-chance variable.
					</p>

				</div>


				<div class="question-card">

					<span class="question-number">
						02
					</span>

					<h3>
						Does a neural network outperform logistic regression on the same features?
					</h3>

					<p>
						Train both models under a common train-validation
						split and compare their predictive behavior using
						classification metrics.
					</p>

				</div>


				<div class="question-card question-wide">

					<span class="question-number">
						03
					</span>

					<h3>
						What does additional model complexity actually buy us?
					</h3>

					<p>
						Compare predictive performance against the added
						training and modeling complexity of the neural-network
						approach.
					</p>

				</div>

			</div>

		</div>

	</div>


	<!-- =====================================================
	     04 — DATA
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			Data
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<p class="eyebrow">
				Structured applicant profiles
			</p>

			<h2>
				Seven inputs, one prediction task.
			</h2>

			<p>
				The analysis uses the Graduate Admissions dataset and
				constructs the prediction problem from seven applicant-level
				features.
			</p>


			<div class="feature-grid">

				<div>
					<span>01</span>
					<strong>GRE Score</strong>
				</div>

				<div>
					<span>02</span>
					<strong>TOEFL Score</strong>
				</div>

				<div>
					<span>03</span>
					<strong>University Rating</strong>
				</div>

				<div>
					<span>04</span>
					<strong>SOP</strong>
				</div>

				<div>
					<span>05</span>
					<strong>LOR</strong>
				</div>

				<div>
					<span>06</span>
					<strong>CGPA</strong>
				</div>

				<div>
					<span>07</span>
					<strong>Research</strong>
				</div>

			</div>


			<div class="target-card">

				<div class="target-number">
					Target
				</div>

				<div>

					<h3>
						High admission chance
					</h3>

					<p>
						The continuous <em>Chance of Admit</em> variable
						is converted into a binary classification target
						using a threshold of 0.80.
					</p>

				</div>

			</div>

		</div>

	</div>


	<!-- =====================================================
	     05 — PREPROCESSING
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			Preparation
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<p class="eyebrow">
				Keeping the comparison consistent
			</p>

			<h2>
				A shared experimental setup.
			</h2>

			<p>
				The models use the same seven predictors and the same
				train-validation partition so their performance can be
				compared on equal footing.
			</p>


			<div class="setup-grid">

				<div>

					<span>
						Split
					</span>

					<strong>
						80 / 20
					</strong>

					<p>
						Training and validation split with stratification
						on the binary target.
					</p>

				</div>


				<div>

					<span>
						Scaling
					</span>

					<strong>
						StandardScaler
					</strong>

					<p>
						Features are standardized before model fitting.
					</p>

				</div>


				<div>

					<span>
						Evaluation
					</span>

					<strong>
						Accuracy + AUC
					</strong>

					<p>
						Classification performance is evaluated using
						accuracy and ROC-AUC behavior.
					</p>

				</div>

			</div>

		</div>

	</div>


	<!-- =====================================================
	     06 — MODELS
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			Models
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<p class="eyebrow">
				Complexity versus simplicity
			</p>

			<h2>
				Two approaches to the same problem.
			</h2>


			<div class="model-grid">

				<div class="model-card">

					<div class="model-top">

						<span>
							Model 01
						</span>

						<span>
							Statistical baseline
						</span>

					</div>


					<h3>
						Logistic Regression
					</h3>

					<p>
						A conventional linear classification model provides
						a strong baseline for determining whether a more
						flexible architecture is actually necessary.
					</p>


					<div class="model-tags">

						<span>Linear decision function</span>
						<span>Probability output</span>
						<span>ROC evaluation</span>

					</div>

				</div>


				<div class="model-card">

					<div class="model-top">

						<span>
							Model 02
						</span>

						<span>
							Neural model
						</span>

					</div>


					<h3>
						Artificial Neural Network
					</h3>

					<p>
						A compact feed-forward neural network introduces
						nonlinear transformations while keeping the architecture
						appropriate for a small structured feature space.
					</p>


					<div class="architecture">

						<div>
							<span>Input</span>
							<strong>7</strong>
						</div>

						<div class="architecture-arrow">
							→
						</div>

						<div>
							<span>Hidden</span>
							<strong>4</strong>
						</div>

						<div class="architecture-arrow">
							→
						</div>

						<div>
							<span>Hidden</span>
							<strong>2</strong>
						</div>

						<div class="architecture-arrow">
							→
						</div>

						<div>
							<span>Output</span>
							<strong>1</strong>
						</div>

					</div>


					<div class="model-tags">

						<span>ReLU</span>
						<span>Sigmoid</span>
						<span>Adam</span>
						<span>Binary Cross-Entropy</span>
						<span>Early Stopping</span>

					</div>

				</div>

			</div>

		</div>

	</div>


	<!-- =====================================================
	     07 — RESULTS
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			Results
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<p class="eyebrow">
				Validation performance
			</p>

			<h2>
				The simpler model remained highly competitive.
			</h2>

			<p>
				On the validation split, logistic regression achieved the
				same accuracy as the best neural-network validation accuracy
				recorded during training.
			</p>


			<div class="result-grid">

				<div class="result-card">

					<span class="result-label">
						Logistic Regression
					</span>

					<div class="result-value">
						94%
					</div>

					<p>
						Validation accuracy
					</p>

				</div>


				<div class="result-card">

					<span class="result-label">
						Logistic Regression
					</span>

					<div class="result-value">
						0.976
					</div>

					<p>
						Validation ROC-AUC
					</p>

				</div>


				<div class="result-card">

					<span class="result-label">
						Neural Network
					</span>

					<div class="result-value">
						94%
					</div>

					<p>
						Best validation accuracy
					</p>

				</div>


				<div class="result-card">

					<span class="result-label">
						Neural Network
					</span>

					<div class="result-value">
						90.7%
					</div>

					<p>
						Mean validation accuracy during training
					</p>

				</div>

			</div>


			<div class="result-interpretation">

				<span>
					Interpretation
				</span>

				<p>
					For this dataset and experimental setup, the neural
					network did not demonstrate a clear validation-accuracy
					advantage over logistic regression.
				</p>

				<p>
					The result illustrates an important modeling principle:
					a more flexible model is not automatically a better model,
					particularly for relatively low-dimensional structured data.
				</p>

			</div>

		</div>

	</div>


	<!-- =====================================================
	     08 — PIPELINE
	===================================================== -->

